This vignette shows a compact R workflow for quality control of a quantitative
proteomics table. The example file contains protein identifiers, intensity
columns, and q-value columns.

```{r load-input}
library(complexbrowser)

input_file <- system.file("extdata", "examples", "myo.csv", package = "complexbrowser")
input <- read.csv(input_file, check.names = FALSE)

n_conditions <- 6
n_replicates <- 3

dim(input)
names(input)[1:10]
```

The first column contains protein identifiers. The next `n_conditions *
n_replicates` columns contain the quantitative values. In this file the
replicates are grouped by condition, so the first three quantitative columns
belong to condition 1, the next three to condition 2, and so on.

```{r plot-input-distribution}
measurement_columns <- 2:(n_conditions * n_replicates + 1)
measurements <- as.matrix(input[, measurement_columns])

boxplot(
  log2(measurements),
  las = 2,
  ylab = "log2 intensity",
  main = "Sample intensity distributions"
)
```

Run the QC workflow. `log2 = FALSE` means that the intensity values are on the
raw scale. `q_values = FALSE` calculates q-values instead of reading them from
the input file. `statistic = "polystest"` uses the combined PolySTest FDR; use
`statistic = "limma"` when the limma FDR returned by PolySTest is wanted.

```{r run-qc}
qc <- complexbrowser_run_qc(
  data = input,
  no_cond = n_conditions,
  no_rep = n_replicates,
  log2 = FALSE,
  grouped = FALSE,
  q_values = FALSE,
  normalization = NULL,
  design = "unpaired",
  statistic = "polystest"
)

qc$metadata
dim(qc$merged_table)
```
 
The result contains the prepared input table, per-protein statistics, a merged
table for export, and metadata. The statistics list is the object used by the
complex-analysis workflow.

```{r inspect-statistics}
qc_columns <- c(
  "ProteinID",
  "Mean intensity C1",
  "Mean intensity C2",
  "Condition_1_CV",
  "Condition_2_CV",
  "qValue C2",
  "Fold change C2/C1"
)

head(qc$merged_table[, qc_columns])
```

Coefficient of variation helps identify conditions with broad replicate spread.

```{r plot-cv}
boxplot(
  qc$stats$CV_df,
  las = 2,
  ylab = "CV (%)",
  main = "Coefficient of variation by condition"
)
```

Fold change and q-value can be inspected together for a condition-versus-control
comparison. Here, condition 2 is compared with condition 1.

```{r plot-qvalue-fold-change}
plot(
  qc$stats$log2_ratios[, "Log2(ratio) C2/C1"],
  -log10(qc$stats$qValue_df[, "qValue C2"]),
  pch = 16,
  cex = 0.4,
  xlab = "log2 ratio C2/C1",
  ylab = "-log10 q-value",
  main = "Condition 2 versus condition 1"
)
abline(v = c(-1, 1), lty = 2, col = "grey50")
abline(h = -log10(0.05), lty = 2, col = "grey50")
```

The merged table can be written for another tool, while the full R object can be
saved when downstream ComplexBrowser analysis should be reproducible.

```{r write-output, eval=FALSE}
utils::write.csv(qc$merged_table, file = "myo-qc-statistics.csv", row.names = FALSE)
saveRDS(qc, file = "myo-qc-result.rds")
```
